WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:04.770
Good afternoon everyone.

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:08.050
Welcome to our latest webinar on API keys.

00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:13.280
We have been talking about API keys for a
while and it's been an interesting journey

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:14.650
for all of us.

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:20.210
We learned a lot from talking with all of
you about keys and your use cases and requirements.

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:24.880
We are now prepared to move forward with implementation.

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:29.839
If you have not had a chance to look at our
previous webinars, the most recent was in

00:00:29.839 --> 00:00:36.030
April of this year, I encourage you to go
to our webinar site on the main NCBI home

00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:40.220
page, click on Learn and webinars and you
will find them there.

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:41.220
Also on the YouTube site.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:44.280
You can catch up on what we talked about before.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:47.270
I won't repeat all the information.

00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:49.270
It's all available to you.

00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:54.290
I want to do a quick review to make sure we
are at least on the basic page which is similar

00:00:54.290 --> 00:00:55.470
of understanding.

00:00:55.470 --> 00:01:03.671
We will talk about the current sandbox in
place now for you to test the keys.

00:01:03.671 --> 00:01:08.110
We will hammer home what is happening now
as far as the status and what you should expect.

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:17.370
Also what is coming, testing periods and the
full release of functional keys in production.

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:21.320
This is a reminder of what the E-Utilities
do.

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:27.930
The point of the slide is to confirm the fact
this is only applied to E-Utilities and not

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:34.720
any other APIs at NCBI, such as the BLAST
or variation services API.

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:42.020
It just affects the E-utilities but it affects
all of them, esarch, esummary, efetch, elink,

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:43.020
epost.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:51.610
These are unique strings that identify either
a user or piece of software to our APIs.

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:58.170
Keys need to be included in every API request
and they are attached to an NCBI account,

00:01:58.170 --> 00:01:59.500
one key per account.

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:07.940
The idea is that with a key we will enable
you to have increased access to servers in

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:14.590
exchange for some information about usage
with the servers and your software.

00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:19.260
It will help us all make APIs more stable
and reliable.

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:22.780
They are freely available to the public.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:30.370
Free without any regulation leads to overuse
and abuse at times, so we like we would have

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:37.370
them available 24/7 fairly and openly so establishing
keys let's us monitor that and make sure we

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:41.170
can provide that level of service for everyone.

00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:45.930
You get a key very simply by going to NCBI
account.

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:51.879
You may know this as a MyNCBI account, it's
the same thing, but you go and log in and

00:02:51.879 --> 00:02:57.140
there will be a button which says create an
API key on the main page of your account.

00:02:57.140 --> 00:03:03.599
If you click it you get a key which is a string
and copy it and put it in your code.

00:03:03.599 --> 00:03:06.689
You have one key per NCBI account.

00:03:06.689 --> 00:03:10.170
If you have multiple keys you need to make
multiple NCBI accounts.

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:12.610
How do you use it?

00:03:12.610 --> 00:03:14.080
It's pretty simple.

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:21.480
In all the E-utilities there is a parameter
called &amp;api underscore key, and you assign

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:27.510
your key to that parameter and you put that
in all your different requests and you are

00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:28.510
done.

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:29.950
That's all you have to do.

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:34.590
If you are an EDirect user there is an environmental
variable named NCBI underscore API underscore

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:37.150
KEY and I show it on the screen.

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:40.739
You set that environmental variable to your
key and you are done.

00:03:40.739 --> 00:03:42.690
You don't have to do anything else.

00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:48.680
EDirect is intelligent and insert your key
into all requests you have.

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:54.779
If you don't like your key and think someone
has stolen it, whatever the issue, you can

00:03:54.779 --> 00:04:01.879
replace it but remember if you do that your
old key is immediately deactivated so no chance

00:04:01.879 --> 00:04:07.680
of you having more than one key on it account
at any particular time.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:12.090
What is available to test is what we call
the sandbox.

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:18.280
The sandbox has the URL you see on the screen.

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:24.040
https://api.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/eutils/
If you use this sandbox, you will find your

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:26.390
keys are always active.

00:04:26.390 --> 00:04:31.790
They are active in the way that they will
be once they are fully in production.

00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:38.070
This is a great way to test the way your keys
are acting, how your software is managing

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:43.460
the keys and managing error responses they
receive from the keys.

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:49.010
I placed a couple example URLs at the bottom
of the screen.

00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:54.530
https://api.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/eutils/esearch.fcgi?db=pubmed&amp;term=lung
https://api.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/eutils/efetch.fcgi?db=protein&amp;id=15718680

00:04:54.530 --> 00:04:59.610
All you have to do is that and provide your
API key and you are running on the sandbox.

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:04.100
It's available now and has been available
the last coiple months.

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:07.250
Please use that as you want to test.

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:12.190
Just to confirm what actually is happening
now because I know this can be confusing,

00:05:12.190 --> 00:05:15.670
here is a basic summary.

00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:23.280
If you are in the API domain, so that is the
sandbox, if you don't have a key included

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:31.280
in your request and your current rate limit
is three requests per second for an IP address.

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:38.870
If there is no key in the request it looks
at the IP address and if any IP address gives

00:05:38.870 --> 00:05:45.760
us more than three requests per second, then
that IP address will receive a status 429

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:47.410
error.

00:05:47.410 --> 00:05:53.440
If you are in the sandbox and have a key in
your request and include it in the URL, then

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:57.720
your rate limit will be whatever value is
assigned to that key.

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:01.320
The default value is 10 requests per second.

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:06.800
If you don't do anything you will get a limit
of 10.

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:12.650
If you have negotiated a higher limit with
us, and as I show you on the screen you can

00:06:12.650 --> 00:06:23.210
write to us at info@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov you can
negotiate a higher rate than 10 . So if you

00:06:23.210 --> 00:06:29.580
negotiate to whatever it is, that is the rate
you should see.

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:32.030
What's happening in production?

00:06:32.030 --> 00:06:36.440
Basically there are no limits that are key
based on production at all.

00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:39.630
Whether you are using a key or not.

00:06:39.630 --> 00:06:44.380
You can include your key in production requests
but they are not functional at all at this

00:06:44.380 --> 00:06:45.580
point.

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:50.680
So production has no effective limits based
on keys and the sandbox does.

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:57.310
The basic idea is when we go into full production,
the behavior of the sandbox now transfers

00:06:57.310 --> 00:06:59.060
to full production.

00:06:59.060 --> 00:07:04.901
So IP addresses without keys will have a limit
of three requests per second and if there

00:07:04.901 --> 00:07:12.760
is a key present then you get the assigned
rate limit and the default is 10.

00:07:12.760 --> 00:07:18.340
Again, another way of reviewing this is if
you don't have a key in the sandbox any IP

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:23.080
that request more than three requests per
second will receive a status 429 error.

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:30.170
If you have a key then by default it is 10
requests per second for that key.

00:07:30.170 --> 00:07:33.940
Keep in mind it is any activity around that
key.

00:07:33.940 --> 00:07:40.430
If you have multiple people using the same
key, it is the sum of all of the activity

00:07:40.430 --> 00:07:43.470
has to be below your rate limit.

00:07:43.470 --> 00:07:48.800
So if you have five people, five machines
using the same key, then the sum of the activity

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:55.420
together has to be no more than your rate
limit given that the default is 10.

00:07:55.420 --> 00:07:58.400
We measure that rate in one second windows.

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:03.530
So it's a one second moving window and not
a blockage or permanent.

00:08:03.530 --> 00:08:09.680
Service is restored immediately whenever the
amount of traffic coming in falls below the

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:11.120
rate limit.

00:08:11.120 --> 00:08:14.710
So there is no action you need to take if
you receive an error.

00:08:14.710 --> 00:08:20.730
You should have service restored immediately
once the rate falls below the limit.

00:08:20.730 --> 00:08:23.520
What will we be doing soon?

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:30.280
We are going to be setting up testing periods
where the behavior on sandbox will transfer

00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:33.010
to production for one hour.

00:08:33.010 --> 00:08:40.189
We will be announcing them on utilities announce,
social media and also the Insights blog.

00:08:40.189 --> 00:08:45.069
There already has been a blog post I will
show you at the end of the webinar where you

00:08:45.069 --> 00:08:49.810
can look at these and when the periods will
be.

00:08:49.810 --> 00:08:54.959
During the one hour periods when the behavior
production is live it will be under full release

00:08:54.959 --> 00:08:55.999
conditions.

00:08:55.999 --> 00:08:59.199
So identical to the sandbox.

00:08:59.199 --> 00:09:00.589
The money slide is this.

00:09:00.589 --> 00:09:04.839
I will put it up for a little time so you
can look at it.

00:09:04.839 --> 00:09:06.620
This is when the test periods will be.

00:09:06.620 --> 00:09:08.330
There are three of them.

00:09:08.330 --> 00:09:15.649
The first one is this Saturday, September
15 from 6:00 p.m. until 7:00 p.m. Eastern

00:09:15.649 --> 00:09:16.649
daylight Time.

00:09:16.649 --> 00:09:21.510
I also put times up for other cities around
the world so you get the idea.

00:09:21.510 --> 00:09:27.120
The next period is September 20 and then September
25 and the times are listed.

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:32.189
It is at 10 AM on September 20 and 2 AM on
September 25.

00:09:32.189 --> 00:09:39.350
The strategy is to catch a variety of times
in a 24 hour clock and a variety of different

00:09:39.350 --> 00:09:40.759
days of the week.

00:09:40.759 --> 00:09:46.660
The point of these is to make sure that everyone
is aware of this as much as we possibly can

00:09:46.660 --> 00:09:48.910
make everyone aware.

00:09:48.910 --> 00:09:56.569
For your planning, you can conceive of your
software working under full production conditions

00:09:56.569 --> 00:09:59.450
during these one hour periods.

00:09:59.450 --> 00:10:04.720
So if you have software that has not been
adjusted to accept keys or the limits then

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:06.860
it could be a problem.

00:10:06.860 --> 00:10:14.160
The software could receive 429 errors during
that time if rate limits are exceeded.

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:19.779
So without keys, any IP doing more than three
requests per second will get a 429 error during

00:10:19.779 --> 00:10:21.430
these 24 hour periods.

00:10:21.430 --> 00:10:28.259
If you have a key and go above your limit
you will get 429 errors during these periods.

00:10:28.259 --> 00:10:35.420
We will again announce these a day or two
before they happen to remind everyone that

00:10:35.420 --> 00:10:38.279
those are the testing periods.

00:10:38.279 --> 00:10:43.190
Full release is currently scheduled for December
1 at midnight.

00:10:43.190 --> 00:10:49.760
So on December 1, all the sandbox API keys
will become active in production.

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:55.029
Here is the point that a couple of people
have asked about, if you have keys in the

00:10:55.029 --> 00:10:59.870
sandbox now you do not need to request new
keys.

00:10:59.870 --> 00:11:04.230
Those keys in the sandbox simply transfer
to production.

00:11:04.230 --> 00:11:08.060
So if you have keys and you requested them
and we negotiated rates with you and they

00:11:08.060 --> 00:11:12.399
are in the sandbox and you are done.

00:11:12.399 --> 00:11:15.069
They transfer to production and you don't
have to do anything.

00:11:15.069 --> 00:11:17.589
They become active on 1 December.

00:11:17.589 --> 00:11:23.990
And one final note is we advise everyone to
continue to use the tool and email parameters

00:11:23.990 --> 00:11:26.579
or begin using them if you are not.

00:11:26.579 --> 00:11:31.970
These are parameters that have been available
to you for many years and they are helpful

00:11:31.970 --> 00:11:39.879
in helping us identify the software package
which is the tool value that is being used

00:11:39.879 --> 00:11:44.990
and also an email contact for developer in
case we see a problematic activity and would

00:11:44.990 --> 00:11:51.300
like to get in touch with you to help understand
what might be going on if there is a problem.

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:59.870
So the value of email should be a valid email
for a developer who can actually do something

00:11:59.870 --> 00:12:01.339
about the software.

00:12:01.339 --> 00:12:04.720
The value of tool should be the name of the
software package.

00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:09.589
It's possible to have multiple keys for that
individual software package.

00:12:09.589 --> 00:12:14.230
So tool is a way of us clustering a group
of keys together around a particular software

00:12:14.230 --> 00:12:15.230
tool.

00:12:15.230 --> 00:12:19.120
If you have questions about that or would
like to discuss issues with that, we are happy

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:20.259
to do that.

00:12:20.259 --> 00:12:28.079
Those are useful parameters and we encourage
you to use them or continue using them.

00:12:28.079 --> 00:12:29.910
That's basically it.

00:12:29.910 --> 00:12:34.330
I would like to encourage you again to follow
our social media for announcements of the

00:12:34.330 --> 00:12:36.930
testing periods as they come along.

00:12:36.930 --> 00:12:42.029
There is a blog post I have linked here which
is the second link that describes almost everything

00:12:42.029 --> 00:12:46.779
I have just told you including listing times
for the testing periods, including the December

00:12:46.779 --> 00:12:51.149
1 date and another things to be aware of.

00:12:51.149 --> 00:12:56.410
You can look at the webinars on our learn
page.

00:12:56.410 --> 00:12:59.000
Subscribe to the utilities announcements if
you haven't already.

00:12:59.000 --> 00:13:07.470
We will post all the announcements to utilities-announce
so it's a great place to get updates on APIs.

00:13:07.470 --> 00:13:12.720
If you would like to talk with us in more
detail please write to info@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:17.639
and if you would like to negotiate a higher
rate key with us or have any questions about

00:13:17.639 --> 00:13:26.000
the keys we are happy to set up a call with
you if it would be helpful to discuss it further.

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:34.010
I will be quiet at this point and let Peter
and Dany let us know what you had questions

00:13:34.010 --> 00:13:35.010
about.

00:13:35.010 --> 00:13:36.959
[Peter Cooper] This is Peter.

00:13:36.959 --> 00:13:40.660
We have a few questions I would like for Eric
to weigh in on.

00:13:40.660 --> 00:13:45.601
I answered a couple of them but we want to
make sure you get a chance to address them.

00:13:45.601 --> 00:13:53.130
One is someone got the impression or saw in
the sandbox it was limit set to 100 instead

00:13:53.130 --> 00:13:54.639
of 10.

00:13:54.639 --> 00:13:57.290
Is that a bug or are you aware of that?

00:13:57.290 --> 00:14:03.069
[Eric Sayers] There was a temporary bug in
the code where some keys were set to 100.

00:14:03.069 --> 00:14:08.300
They should be set to 10 at this point and
that should be either fixed now or in the

00:14:08.300 --> 00:14:09.769
process of being fixed.

00:14:09.769 --> 00:14:16.670
So there were some cases where the rate limit
was 100 mistakenly for some keys.

00:14:16.670 --> 00:14:19.129
If you saw that, that is likely what was happening.

00:14:19.129 --> 00:14:25.059
My understanding is that we understand the
problem and have corrected it and should be

00:14:25.059 --> 00:14:27.110
pushed out.

00:14:27.110 --> 00:14:33.350
Contact us if you are having issues with that
or just in general if you see a rate limit

00:14:33.350 --> 00:14:40.160
or apparent rate limit that is not consistent
with what it should be, that is likely a problem

00:14:40.160 --> 00:14:42.889
so please let us know about that.

00:14:42.889 --> 00:14:49.579
[Peter] Another question and I think I know
the answer but want you to confirm it is true,

00:14:49.579 --> 00:14:53.050
someone asks how can you see Your key if you
forget it ? I believe it's visible in your

00:14:53.050 --> 00:14:54.209
NCBI account.

00:14:54.209 --> 00:14:55.370
[Eric] Absolutely.

00:14:55.370 --> 00:15:00.149
You just log into your NCBI account and go
to the account page.

00:15:00.149 --> 00:15:06.629
Instead of having a button that says create
an API key, your key will simply be displayed.

00:15:06.629 --> 00:15:11.429
It's displayed there all the time so it should
not be an issue.

00:15:11.429 --> 00:15:15.059
[Peter] A couple of things, this is an interesting
question.

00:15:15.059 --> 00:15:19.709
Someone wants to know what you mean by the
testing period?

00:15:19.709 --> 00:15:24.410
We want people to try it and make sure they
know what workflows will be like and comment

00:15:24.410 --> 00:15:25.410
on that.

00:15:25.410 --> 00:15:28.600
[Eric] A couple of reasons for having the
testing period.

00:15:28.600 --> 00:15:34.949
One is we would expect that you would want
to make sure that your software works under

00:15:34.949 --> 00:15:38.850
the API keys in production.

00:15:38.850 --> 00:15:43.360
Many of you have set up your software to hit
our sandbox URLs.

00:15:43.360 --> 00:15:48.130
These testing periods allow you to have your
software hit production URLs with the keys

00:15:48.130 --> 00:15:49.639
in place.

00:15:49.639 --> 00:15:56.370
If it was me, I would look forward to those
one hour periods to run software through the

00:15:56.370 --> 00:16:02.040
production E-utilities API service and load
it as you think it might be loaded to make

00:16:02.040 --> 00:16:05.639
sure your keys are appropriate and you aren't
getting errors.

00:16:05.639 --> 00:16:10.329
If you need higher rates, that might be an
indicator that you need to talk to us so we

00:16:10.329 --> 00:16:11.899
can adjust them.

00:16:11.899 --> 00:16:15.759
That is the one way you can think about it.

00:16:15.759 --> 00:16:21.110
The other and our experience with other releases
like this is we are trying to make sure people

00:16:21.110 --> 00:16:25.449
are aware something is happening, and to be
frank, this is a way we get some people's

00:16:25.449 --> 00:16:26.449
attention.

00:16:26.449 --> 00:16:29.029
There are two main goals.

00:16:29.029 --> 00:16:34.019
We want to increase awareness that something
is happening and also trying to provide an

00:16:34.019 --> 00:16:41.129
opportunity for developers to actually have
their software work in the production environment

00:16:41.129 --> 00:16:42.569
as it will be after December 1.

00:16:42.569 --> 00:16:48.730
[Peter] There are two questions about negotiating
an increased rate.

00:16:48.730 --> 00:16:53.339
I know you said this but can you remind people
how to go about it?

00:16:53.339 --> 00:16:54.810
And what is involved?

00:16:54.810 --> 00:16:58.139
Is there a cost involved?

00:16:58.139 --> 00:17:01.700
How do we decide whether we will give them
that were not?

00:17:01.700 --> 00:17:06.189
[Eric] We are all in the public domain.

00:17:06.189 --> 00:17:08.380
Everything is freely available.

00:17:08.380 --> 00:17:12.390
There is no increased cost or anything like
that because there is no cost.

00:17:12.390 --> 00:17:19.870
What we look at is the level of activity that
your software is producing and that we are

00:17:19.870 --> 00:17:21.260
seeing.

00:17:21.260 --> 00:17:27.260
So we want to make sure that the API key rate
limit that you have in place is suitable for

00:17:27.260 --> 00:17:30.560
the level of activity that you have.

00:17:30.560 --> 00:17:35.789
So if it's more than 10 we can help you understand
the amount of activity that we are seeing

00:17:35.789 --> 00:17:37.600
from your software.

00:17:37.600 --> 00:17:43.340
So if you have tool and email in place than
we can look at the activity associated with

00:17:43.340 --> 00:17:48.320
those values in tool and email and report
back to you averages that we see as well as

00:17:48.320 --> 00:17:53.240
a maximum rate that we see over perhaps a
month.

00:17:53.240 --> 00:17:56.370
We can talk with you in more detail about
your particular application.

00:17:56.370 --> 00:18:02.220
We can provide some limited usage data to
you to let you know what we are seeing.

00:18:02.220 --> 00:18:09.120
What we are seeing is what we will use to
establish a rate limit error 429.

00:18:09.120 --> 00:18:13.830
We simply want to negotiate with you about
what you are seeing, what we are seeing and

00:18:13.830 --> 00:18:15.740
what we think is a reasonable rate.

00:18:15.740 --> 00:18:20.190
Another thing that has happened in conversation
is we discovered together there might be other

00:18:20.190 --> 00:18:27.780
ways you can reach the goals you have for
your software that is not the engineering

00:18:27.780 --> 00:18:28.970
that you have now in API.

00:18:28.970 --> 00:18:32.830
There might be a more efficient way of doing
what you are trying to do and we might be

00:18:32.830 --> 00:18:38.640
able to explore alternative solutions with
you that you could try that might reduce the

00:18:38.640 --> 00:18:42.990
amount of calls you need to make which is
good for everyone.

00:18:42.990 --> 00:18:44.669
So it's a conversation.

00:18:44.669 --> 00:18:48.970
[Peter] Would you remind them how to begin
the conversation?

00:18:48.970 --> 00:18:50.789
[Eric] hanks Peter, I missed on that.

00:18:50.789 --> 00:18:58.390
You write to info@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov and say
what you would like to do.

00:18:58.390 --> 00:19:06.799
And if you have a value and tool provide those
and we will get back to you and start a conversation.

00:19:06.799 --> 00:19:12.580
[Peter] That email address is on the slide
being displayed right now.

00:19:12.580 --> 00:19:14.340
A couple of other things.

00:19:14.340 --> 00:19:16.610
We have the two URLs now.

00:19:16.610 --> 00:19:20.980
We have the api URL and the eutils URL.

00:19:20.980 --> 00:19:27.290
Once we go to production, which one will we
retain a which goes away and those kind of

00:19:27.290 --> 00:19:28.290
things?

00:19:28.290 --> 00:19:36.520
[Eric] The api.ncbi is only a sandbox and
we won't make that into a production URL going

00:19:36.520 --> 00:19:37.520
forward.

00:19:37.520 --> 00:19:39.910
The production URLs going forward are not
changing.

00:19:39.910 --> 00:19:46.000
You would still use eutils.ncbi the same URLs
you have been using.

00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:50.500
There are no plans at the moment to decommission
the sandbox.

00:19:50.500 --> 00:19:56.669
I suspect at some point after December 1 once
we see everything has transitioned over correctly

00:19:56.669 --> 00:20:01.350
we may decide to decommission sandbox because
it will not be of much use.

00:20:01.350 --> 00:20:06.900
Let us know if you would like to have a sandbox
available, we can have it available for more

00:20:06.900 --> 00:20:07.990
time.

00:20:07.990 --> 00:20:13.120
Our expectation is that production and sandbox
will be exactly the same experience from the

00:20:13.120 --> 00:20:14.419
point of view of the keys.

00:20:14.419 --> 00:20:19.640
And I know this question has come up in other
contexts, but the api URL at this point will

00:20:19.640 --> 00:20:25.260
not be the production supported URL for the
E-utilities at this point after December 1.

00:20:25.260 --> 00:20:27.890
It will remain a test sandbox.

00:20:27.890 --> 00:20:34.230
[Peter] Another one, someone is wondering
if there will be an API for managing the API

00:20:34.230 --> 00:20:35.230
keys.

00:20:35.230 --> 00:20:36.409
[Eric] That's a great question.

00:20:36.409 --> 00:20:40.889
At this point, point, there is not an API
to manage the keys . It something that's come

00:20:40.889 --> 00:20:45.840
up in conversations and the best I can say
is we are thinking about it but no immediate

00:20:45.840 --> 00:20:46.840
plans.

00:20:46.840 --> 00:20:51.020
[Peter] I think this is the last one that
we will address.

00:20:51.020 --> 00:20:57.460
Someone asks because they are using the API
key now, is that basically being ignored or

00:20:57.460 --> 00:21:02.769
is it a problem if they use it before the
test period, their software uses the API key

00:21:02.769 --> 00:21:03.769
now?

00:21:03.769 --> 00:21:04.840
[Eric] I salute you for doing it.

00:21:04.840 --> 00:21:07.600
You are doing exactly what you should be doing.

00:21:07.600 --> 00:21:09.720
There is no problem with having the API key.

00:21:09.720 --> 00:21:16.070
It is basically ignored in production currently
but great you have it there because now you

00:21:16.070 --> 00:21:17.070
are done and don't have to do anything.

00:21:17.070 --> 00:21:22.110
That is what we recommend is once you get
your keys and are happy with their performance

00:21:22.110 --> 00:21:27.210
in the sandbox and have a rate you are comfortable
with, put them in production and release the

00:21:27.210 --> 00:21:29.740
code and let it go so you are ready for December.

00:21:29.740 --> 00:21:35.120
[Peter] I think that's all the questions we
can address right now.

00:21:35.120 --> 00:21:40.950
Certainly if you have additional questions
you can write to us at info@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov

00:21:40.950 --> 00:21:47.940
. We will have the questions written up with
the answers that we gave in the webinar and

00:21:47.940 --> 00:21:49.480
maybe some additional information.

00:21:49.480 --> 00:21:53.440
It will be available in the materials directory.

00:21:53.440 --> 00:21:54.440
https://go.usa.gov/xUdrQ

00:21:54.440 --> 00:21:56.429
I think that will be the end of the webinar
for today.

00:21:56.429 --> 00:21:58.100
We will see you all next time.

